course: dictionary
lesson: 2036
title: Confusion: the feeling of not knowing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Confusion is what you feel when you do not understand something yet, or when things are mixed up and nobody knows what is happening. «In the confusion, the cook disappears.» Here the kitchen is busy and messy, and nobody knows where the cook has gone.
explain: Confusion can also be a helpful signal. In school, feeling confused means you have not understood yet, so ask a question. Not every mix-up is confusion, though. «Children growing up with two languages often mix them in one sentence, and this is normal, not confusion.» Mixing words can be a normal way of talking.
explain: Confusion can be a warning sign. «Signs of concussion include headache, dizziness, feeling sick, blurred vision, confusion, forgetting things and wobbling.» If someone has a bump on the head and is confused, tell an adult straight away.
fact: Confusion means not understanding something, or a messy state where things are mixed up.
fact: Confusion can also mean mistaking one person or thing for another.
fact: Useful phrases: «in confusion», «cause confusion», «to avoid confusion», «there was some confusion about», «in all the confusion».
fact: «There was some confusion about» means people were not sure what was true or what had been agreed.
fact: Feeling confused in a lesson is normal, and asking a question is the right thing to do.
exercise: «In the confusion, the cook disappears.» What does confusion mean here? | A special recipe for a cake | *A messy time when things are mixed up and nobody knows what is happening | A quiet, calm kitchen with everything in its place | A food that tastes very strange
exercise: Maya is stuck on a maths sum and says, «I am in confusion.» What does she mean? | I have lost my pencil case | *I do not understand it yet, and I need to ask | I have finished and checked every answer | I like maths more than art
exercise: After a bump on the head, which sign means you must tell an adult straight away? | Feeling thirsty after running | Wanting a snack | A little bit of tiredness after school | *Confusion, not knowing what is happening
test: «Children growing up with two languages often mix them in one sentence; this is normal, not confusion.» Why is mixing two languages not confusion? | Because it is always a mistake | Because the children are too tired to speak | Because it means the same as being asleep | *Because it is a normal way of talking, not a sign of being lost
test: Which word could replace confusion in «in all the confusion»? | Calm | Happiness | *Mix-up | Quiet
exam: «There was some confusion about the time of the match.» What does confusion mean in this sentence? | Being very happy about the match | A large crowd at the ground | *Not being sure, with things mixed up | A bright, sunny afternoon
